  │            │ the stored JSON shape.                                                             │                │
  ├────────────┼────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┼────────────────┤
  │ [38;5;153mapp.js[39m     │ DOM wiring only: read the form, validate, call auth, hand the result to session,   │ both           │
  │            │ report to the user.                                                                │                │
  └────────────┴────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┴────────────────┘

  [38;5;153mvalidateForm[39m stays in [38;5;153mapp.js[39m — it validates form input, which is DOM-layer work.

  [1mThe auth response shape[0m — this is the contract real auth will have to satisfy, so it's worth getting right now:

  [32m// success[39m
  { [36mok[39m: [34mtrue[39m,  [36muser[39m: { [36mid[39m: [31m"u_3f9a2c"[39m, [36musername[39m: [31m"alice"[39m } }
  [32m// failure (the stub never returns this yet, but the shape exists)[39m
  { [36mok[39m: [34mfalse[39m, [36merror[39m: [31m"Invalid credentials"[39m }

  The stub synthesizes [38;5;153mid[39m deterministically from the username so it's stable across logins in a session. Swapping in
  real auth means replacing the body of one function; the shape callers depend on doesn't move.

  [1mThe stored shape[0m — one key, one JSON object:

  sessionStorage[[31m"webapp.session"[39m] = [31m'{"userId":"u_3f9a2c","username":"alice"}'[39m

  A single namespaced key rather than parallel [38;5;153muserId[39m/[38;5;153musername[39m keys, so the identity is written and cleared atomically
  and adding a field later doesn't add a key. [38;5;153msession.js[39m exposes [38;5;153msaveSession(user)[39m, [38;5;153mgetSession()[39m, [38;5;153mgetUserId()[39m,
  [38;5;153mclearSession()[39m — callers never touch [38;5;153msessionStorage[39m or the key name, which is what makes the store swappable.

  [1mOne thing I need to flag:[0m the repo's [38;5;153msrc/*.js[39m use CommonJS [38;5;153mrequire[39m, and [38;5;153mpackage.json[39m has no [38;5;153m"type"[39m field, so Node
  treats every [38;5;153m.js[39m here as CommonJS — meaning the unit tests can't [38;5;153mimport[39m the new ES modules as-is. My fix is to add
  [38;5;153m"type": "module"[39m to [38;5;153mpackage.json[39m and drop in a two-line [38;5;153msrc/package.json[39m containing [38;5;153m{"type":"commonjs"}[39m, which keeps
  [38;5;153msrc/index.js[39m and [38;5;153msrc/utils.js[39m working [1mwithout editing either file[0m. The browser doesn't read [38;5;153mpackage.json[39m at all, so
  this is purely for Node.

  Does section 1 look right?

